WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.167
Identify the principal. Requests are assigned a deliberate identity key, such as a verified account and endpoint. A spoofable header cannot safely define a protected client budget.

2
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:23.833
Refill the budget. The token bucket refills over elapsed time up to its capacity. Capacity permits a bounded burst while the refill rate controls sustained use.

3
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:34.250
Consume atomically. Checking and consuming a token must behave as one operation for shared state. Separate reads and writes can overspend under concurrency.

4
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:47.667
Allow affordable work. A request with enough tokens proceeds, but still faces authorization, request validation and downstream concurrency limits. Passing the limiter is not a correctness guarantee.

5
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:58.917
Reject excess traffic. A request without budget receives a clear rejection and retry guidance where appropriate. Clients should wait rather than creating an immediate retry storm.

6
00:00:58.917 --> 00:01:11.250
Plan storage failure. If the shared limiter fails, enforce the chosen endpoint policy and alert. Independent local counters do not preserve a strict global quota across replicas.
